"""Deviations D24 onward (retest, 23 October 2026), appended after D23 in Section 12. Draft: numbers that depend on
results still running are filled in when they finish."""
D = [
"D24. Retest timing (23 October 2026). The retest plan was written after every first-run result was known and was approved by the author on 23 October 2026 at 17:52 Dubai time, before any retest exposure was linked to an outcome. The checks run before writing it are listed in retest/prechecks_log.md: variable availability, coverage counts, the share of ideology variance within traditions, and termination-type counts without any exposure. All retest results are exploratory.",
"D25. Matching Equinox to current Seshat polities. The plan described a 60 percent overlap of the shorter span. The frozen script (equinox_match.py) instead requires an overlap of at least half of the current polity's span, and calls a match unique when there is one candidate, or when the best overlap is at least 0.8 and the next below 0.3. Of the current polities, 342 matched uniquely, 11 were ambiguous and 511 had no Equinox counterpart. The 11 ambiguous cases went to a blind AI matcher that saw only names, regions and dates; it matched all 11. The resulting frame has 227 polities with the ideology index (EPI) and 88 with the executive-constraint index (CONSTR).",
"D26. New termination codes. 29 frame polities with an exposure had no termination code. They were coded under Codebook v0.3 by blind AI coders in two batches (G01, G02), with 6 items double-coded by a separate coder; the main ending category (Q2) agreed on all 6, so no adjudication was needed. The shared web-search allowance ran out during both batches, so several items rest on one source or on two pages from one institution. In this first pass two items could not be resolved (Kalingga Kingdom, Susa I: T9 with unknown event status); Susa I drops from the models. A follow-up blind coding of the 8 weakest-sourced items, started after the first analysis run and before its results were read, agreed with the earlier code on 7; for Kalingga it gave continuity where the earlier coder had left the ending unresolved, and a third blind coder also chose continuity, so Kalingga is coded T0. Kalingga has no ideology index, so the Study 1 sample is unchanged; in H8 Part A it adds one polity and no estimate moves by more than 0.02.",
"D27. Century sampling. Covariates are drawn with Seshat's own SeshatDatasetAnalysis package, run offline from its variable template (test_scripts/template.csv) rather than through its API. One fix was needed before freezing: the package stores sampled strings in float columns, so object columns are created first and all-numeric columns are converted back to float. Territory is taken from Cliopatria only for map features that carry a single Seshat polity ID.",
"D28. Agricultural productivity. The Seshat Agri dataset (Turchin et al. 2021, The Holocene 31(6): 1055-1065) was downloaded from seshat-db.com through the author's own account; no account was created for the project. The index is the product of the cropping, fertilizer and irrigation coefficients, taking the nearest dated value in each region and 1 where a coefficient is never recorded. A log transform was dropped because some products are 0. Only the derived index is published, under CC BY-SA 4.0 with the Seshat attribution.",
"D29. H8 Part A additions. The frozen script added two outcomes the plan did not list: ruler transitions from Seshat's power-transitions dataset (irregular if overturn, assassination of the predecessor, military revolt or popular uprising is coded at least 0.9) and log ruler tenure. A secondary exposure, 'ideological thought equates rulers and commoners', is reported beside CONSTR. These were fixed before the script was run.",
"D30. Study 2 motif coding. Reliability on the 487 double-coded motifs met the 0.80 target overall (score alpha 0.865), but not for the sign of bravery (0.78) and fairness (0.77 to 0.80). A sensitivity index dropping these, fixed in the frozen script before the codes were read, is reported. The human check on 281 MTurk-coded trickster motifs found the AI coder agreed with a random worker as often as workers agreed with each other (0.488 against 0.477); human agreement itself was low (Fleiss kappa 0.29).",
"D31. H9a version 2. The sample was enlarged to 53 societies in 42 language families: the 30 of version 1, the 16 further verified candidates, and 7 of 9 candidates from an extra search. The other two were not used because their texts are weak on social life (Cherokee: religion and medicine only; Cayapa: mostly material culture). Coders split on whether blood revenge counts as reciprocity; this was noticed in coder reports before any result was computed, and a post hoc check without revenge passages is reported. Coders M05, M06 and M07 reported file collisions in a shared scratch folder; M06 and M07 rebuilt their codes in private folders, from M17 on every coder had its own folder, and before analysis every first-pass file was checked for the batch's passages in order and for quotes that match their paragraphs exactly. The two review coders for passages the first coder had judged negative saw batches made only of those passages, which may have made confirmation more likely.",
"D32. H9b dated trees. After freezing, a numerical fix was made to phylo.py (logged in phylo_changes_after_freeze.diff): branch lengths are floored at 1e-6, and a non-finite likelihood returns a large penalty, because zero-length edges made the Pagel fit fail. The Mk code was checked on simulated data before use (median estimates 2.13 and 4.28 against true rates 2 and 4, 334 tips).",
"D33. H8 Part D, optional in the plan, was not run: the Jones and Olken assassination-attempt data were not obtained.",
"D34. H6 search and extraction. The search ran under a web-search allowance of 200 calls per turn shared by all agents; Steps 2 to 4 were completed in a second turn, with forward citations and preprint searches run through the OpenAlex index. Many full texts are paywalled or were blocked; studies whose statistics could not be read are listed as missing, never estimated. Rules added before the relevant data were read, in h6_notes.md: the merge rule for the two extractors (h6_merge.py, hashed); the sign of an F statistic is taken from the paper, and an effect whose direction the paper does not state is dropped (S045); a second extraction round for studies found or opened by the completed search, with its own 20 percent verification draw. Only the primary stratum was extracted; the other strata are reported as counts. Two screening decisions were questioned by the search agent and kept as screened (S081 included, S144 excluded).",
"D35. Seshat licence. The repository earlier listed Seshat-derived files as CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. Under the Seshat Databank User Agreement, Public Data are CC BY-SA 4.0. The licence is corrected, and the repository README, the results report and this protocol carry the required attribution: 'This research used data from the Seshat Databank (https://seshat-db.com) under the Creative Commons Attribution–ShareAlike 4.0 International License (CC BY-SA 4.0): https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/.' The Equinox data are cited as Benam et al. (2022), Seshat Data: Equinox Packaged Data (1.0), Zenodo, https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6642229. No Private Data were used or published.",
"D36. Study 1 numerical fix after freezing (23 October 2026). In the first full run, the H2 and H7 estimates were unusable (H2: HR 0 with an infinite interval). The cause was one of the 20 imputation draws (draw 8), in which the default IRLS fit of the complementary log-log model reported convergence at a log-likelihood of -4,375, far below the maximum of -265 that Newton's method finds from the same start. Every hazard fit, and the restricted fit used by the score bootstrap, now also runs Newton's method and keeps the higher likelihood; Newton was used in 3 of 180 fits. The model is unchanged; the diff is in retest/s1r_changes_after_freeze.diff and the new hashes in s1r_frozen.sha256. The rerun also used the final termination codes (D26). A second rerun with the earlier codes agrees to the second decimal on every estimate, and no decision changes. The broken first-run output is kept as s1r_results_run1_broken.json.",
"D37. Independent verification of the retest (23 October 2026). Two agents re-derived the main numbers with their own code, without opening the analysis scripts. Study 1 and H8 Part A: all 38 values reproduced. H9a version 2: every count, alpha and weighted share reproduced, including the property result. H8 Parts B and C: every odds ratio, interval and p-value reproduced. Three points of wording came out of it. (1) The H1 model without region fixed effects keeps all 34 regions; on the main model's 30 regions it gives HR 1.31 [1.01, 1.71]. (2) In H8 Part B, a regime's autocratic share is taken over years whose regime status is known; country-years with neither v2x_regime nor v2x_polyarchy (1,138, mostly before 1900) are left out of the share. Counting them as not autocratic gives 550 rather than 552 regimes at a 10-year lag and OR 0.74 rather than 0.75 unlagged. (3) The Part C model samples differ by one leader from the verifier's, with identical estimates. Durations in Study 1 use the database end year, which differs from the coders' end year in 138 polities, usually by a few years; with the coders' year the region-stratified Cox HR is 1.19 instead of 1.25.",
]
